  "question": {
    "text": "An aqueous solution freezes at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>272</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>,&nbsp;while pure water freezes at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>273</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>,&nbsp;given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>86</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>,&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>512</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo></math> the molality of solution and boiling point of solution respectively will be -",
